scripts.//scripts:
There is deliberately no `prepare`, and that is the whole reason a git install costs nothing here. pnpm decides a git-hosted package must be prepared the moment `prepare` exists — never by looking at whether build output is already present — and preparing means a clone, an inner `npm install` of every devDependency, and the build itself. pnpm 10.26 turned that into a hard stop: ERR_PNPM_GIT_DEP_PREPARE_NOT_ALLOWED until the operator adds a SHA-pinned `allowBuilds` key that has to be rewritten on every commit. Measured on this package with pnpm 11.7.0, that path took 1m32s and installed 95 packages to produce a handful of JavaScript files. A matrix over the lifecycle hooks confirms `prepare` is the only one that triggers it: `prepack`, `prepublishOnly`, `prepublish` and no scripts at all install clean, as do the absolute-path and tarball forms. So `lib/` is committed and the git, absolute-path and tarball installs all resolve straight to it, running no script.

//scripts:prepack is the one build hook that stays, because it is the only one pnpm never runs for an installed package: it fires for `npm pack` and `npm publish`, so a shipped tarball always carries a freshly built lib/, while every install form leaves it alone. `prepublishOnly` is redundant next to it — publishing already runs prepack.

//scripts:verify:artifacts is the guard that replaces the gate pnpm used to provide. With the install no longer rebuilding, a commit that edits src/ without rebuilding would silently ship a stale lib/ to every git installer, so this snapshots lib/, rebuilds, reports every file that differs, and restores the committed tree either way.
